{"name":"Darling Bud","hex":"#ff88ff","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"darling","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Often used as an affectionate term of address: a person who is very dear to one.","A person who is kind, sweet, etc., and thus lovable; a pet, a sweetheart; also, an animal or thing which is cute and lovable."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Very dear; beloved, cherished, favourite.","Very cute or lovable; adorable, charming, sweet."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/darling"},{"term":"bud","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-3 language code for Ntcham."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A newly sprouted leaf or blossom that has not yet unfolded.","Something that has begun to develop."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/bud"}],"entries":[{"title":"Bud","blocks":[{"text":"In botany, a bud is an undeveloped or embryonic shoot and normally occurs in the axil of a leaf or at the tip of a stem. Once formed, a bud may remain for some time in a dormant condition, or it may form a shoot immediately. Buds may be specialized to develop flowers or short shoots or may have the potential for general shoot development. The term bud is also used in zoology, where it refers to an outgrowth from the body which can develop into a new individual."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/ba/Fagus_sylvatica_bud.jpg/330px-Fagus_sylvatica_bud.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bud","wikidata":"Q189838"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/84/Halesia_carolina%2C_skubblare%2C_Manie_van_der_Schijff_BT%2C_a.jpg/500px-Halesia_carolina%2C_skubblare%2C_Manie_van_der_Schijff_BT%2C_a.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/84/Halesia_carolina%2C_skubblare%2C_Manie_van